00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:26.200
MARTIN OMANDER:
Alex is an engineer

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.680
at Vivid, a digital agency
that creates websites

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.110
for their clients using Django.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.020
Django is a web framework
written in Python

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.020
that handles serving
web pages for you.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.930
You define data models
as Python objects,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.090
and Django simplifies
communicating these

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.430
to a database.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.650
Vivid's client websites,
like agate construction,

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.320
currently runs on servers
in Vivid's office.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.670
But the company is
moving to The Cloud.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.310
Alex looked at running their
existing client websites

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.770
on virtual machines,
but that would require

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.810
a fair amount of ops work.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.635
Alex wonders, can
they serverless?

00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:05.010
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
Yeah, they can.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.230
Cloud Run is a managed
serverless platform, where

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.180
each server run stateless.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.820
No data is stored on
the servers itself.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.870
But Cloud Run can
connect to state

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.310
for backing services,
databases, APIs, and the like.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.590
Web frameworks that rely
on databases like Django

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.532
can run in a serverless setup
with some configuration.

00:01:22.532 --> 00:01:24.240
The general steps
described in this video

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.790
can be used for not only
Django, but any other content

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.710
management system
that uses Django,

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.637
such as Wagtail and Django CMS.

00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:32.970
We've created code labs that
walk you through everything

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:33.990
described in this video.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.420
They're linked in the
description below.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.370
Any Django site can be built
into a container image.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.120
This image can be deployed
as a Cloud Run service,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.220
but that just handles
the code part.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.140
Django also needs
a database to store

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.770
relational data and a place to
store media and static assets.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.990
These two backing
services need to be

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.600
provisioned and
accessible by the service.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.820
MARTIN OMANDER: First,
let's talk data.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.470
Django supports many
relational databases,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.220
including PostgreSOL and MySQL.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.790
Alex can use Cloud SQL, a
managed database service,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.895
that supports both PostgreSOL
and MySQL, and even MS SQL.

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:12.270
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: When Alex
first deploys their Cloud Run

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.390
service, they'll need to
set the add SQL instance

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.030
parameter, linking their Cloud
Run service with their Cloud

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.290
SQL database.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.030
This allows the
service in the database

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.860
to communicate over
an automatically

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.330
encrypted connection.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.790
Django will also need
connection settings.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.090
Not just the instance hostname,
but also the database username

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.230
and password.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.870
Alex can store these credentials
using Secret Manager,

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.400
retrieving them at runtime.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.080
From here, everything
is set up to run

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.820
Django's migrate command.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.240
MARTIN OMANDER: Secondly,
media and static assets.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.730
Alex can configure
their new Django app

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.642
to use a Google Cloud Storage
bucket to store these files.

00:02:48.642 --> 00:02:50.100
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN:
Cloud storage can

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.100
be configured as a custom
storage backend in Django using

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.930
the Django storages package.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.000
Alex just needs to
install the package

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.360
and edit their settings.py to
use this new storage backend

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.670
and point to their new
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.010
From here, everything is set
up to run Django's collect

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.330
static command.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.310
MARTIN OMANDER: This
all seems great.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.260
But about those Django commands.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.350
Where do you actually run those?

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.690
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Well, this
is the question, Martin.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.980
Cloud Run focuses on
serving containers,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.620
and doesn't provide a way to
run a one-off shell command

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.570
against a running service.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.640
What Alex can do is
run these commands

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.980
as part of their continuous
integration process

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.750
using Google Cloud Build.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.160
In their Cloud
Build config, they

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.770
can add a new step called
migrate between their built

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.870
step and their deploy step.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.940
This migrate step uses the
App Engine exec wrapper,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.180
which allows commands to
be run against a database

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.160
during a Cloud Build job.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.200
Additionally, they
can configure this job

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.110
to be automatically run
anytime the agate code is

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.920
updated in source control
using Cloud Build triggers.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.690
If an automated process doesn't
work for Alex's migration

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.570
strategy, they can run their
migration commands manually

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.790
with help from Cloud SQL proxy.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.790
We've described this method in
more detail in our blog post.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.040
MARTIN OMANDER: By connecting
these manager's services

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.220
together, Alex is able to host
their websites in the cloud.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.030
These services automatically
scale up and down

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.070
as their load changes
throughout the day,

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.417
without having to pay a fixed
price for a virtual server.

00:04:14.417 --> 00:04:16.500
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Details
for everything mentioned

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.480
in this video,
including the code labs,

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.860
are linked in the description.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.120
So how are the herd doing?

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.843
MARTIN OMANDER: It looks like
they're going along great.

00:04:25.843 --> 00:04:26.760
KATIE MCLAUGHLIN: Yay.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

